# Keep shell scripts with Unix line endings, always.
#
# This is not optional. A .sh file with CRLF endings is broken on Linux:
# bash reads "set -u\r" as an invalid option and "cd /home/container\r" as a
# directory that doesn't exist, so the script dies on line one and the error
# points at the wrong thing entirely.
#
# core.autocrlf=true (the Windows default) rewrites LF to CRLF on checkout,
# which is exactly what broke the startup script. These rules override it for
# anything that has to run on the server.
*.sh text eol=lf

# The same applies to the JSON the bot reads and the JavaScript it runs.
# Node handles CRLF fine, but keeping them LF means the files the server runs
# are byte-identical to the ones in git.
*.js text eol=lf
*.mjs text eol=lf
*.json text eol=lf

# Anything else: let git work it out, but don't try to be clever with binaries.
* text=auto
